msgid "Programming Basics"
msgstr ""

msgid "Assuming that you have some experience programming earlier, in this section we will recap on basic programming concepts *using* **C++**. Since we assume your first exposure to programming was in C, we may compare C++ to C."
msgstr ""

msgid "Basic Structure of a C++ Program"
msgstr ""

#, python-brace-format
msgid "In {numref}`hello-program`, we show an example of a C++ program that prints `Hello world!`."
msgstr ""

msgid "Structure of a C++ program"
msgstr ""

msgid "A simple hello world program showing the structure of a C++ program"
msgstr ""

msgid "`#include <iostream>` is an include statement that includes the standard input-output stream library. `iostream` has implementations of *streams*, which are a sequence of characters transferred between the program and input-output devices. We will use `cout` and `cin`, where `cout` is for output and `cin` is for input. The equivalent of `#include <iostream>` is `#include <stdio.h>` in C."
msgstr ""

msgid "`using namespace std;` is a statement that declares using a container of names named `std`. Here, `namespace` means a container and `std` is the container we will use. `std` is short for standard."
msgstr ""

msgid "`int main (){` is the `main` function which is the first function called when executing our program."
msgstr ""

msgid "`cout` is a standard output stream that we use to write output to the screen."
msgstr ""

msgid "`<<` is an operator. Just like `+` is an operator for addition, `<<` is an operator to output."
msgstr ""

msgid "`\"Hello world!\"` is a string that we want to output. Note that we have it between double quotes."
msgstr ""

msgid "`<< endl;` is the output operator `<<` again followed by `endl`, which is used to output a newline in C++."
msgstr ""

msgid "`return 0;` exits the main function just as in C."
msgstr ""

